\title[The 3SUM--Birthday-Bound Hierarchy]{The 3SUM--Birthday-Bound Hierarchy:\\
Collision Factoring and Three Independent $\sqrt{N}$ Barriers}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We establish a structural bridge between the $3$SUM problem of fine-grained
complexity and integer factoring, and then determine exactly how much that
bridge can carry. The bridge is a \emph{reveal lemma}: if $N=pq$ is a semiprime
and $s$ satisfies $0<s<N$ and $p\mid s$, then $\gcd(s,N)=p$; the side condition
$q\nmid s$ usually imposed is redundant, being implied by $s<N$. Applied to
$s=a+b+c$, a $3$SUM instance solved modulo an unknown prime factor of $N$
yields that factor by one greatest-common-divisor computation.

Collision-based factoring schemes---sumset ($a+b\equiv c+d$), $3$SUM
($a+b+c\equiv d+e+f$), general $r$-SUM, and evaluation schemes with structured
value sets---form an apparent hierarchy in which guaranteeing a collision needs
only $k\approx p^{1/r}$ stored elements, so the exponent improves from $1/2$ to
$1/3$ and beyond. Our main theorem shows the hierarchy collapses: for a finite
search space $S$ and modulus $p\ge 1$, a collision is guaranteed against every
residue map into $\{0,\dots,p-1\}$ \emph{if and only if} $p<\lvert S\rvert$.
The criterion mentions only the number of inspected objects and never the
arity; the threshold is exactly $p+1$ for every scheme, and since the larger
factor of $N=pq$ satisfies $p\ge\sqrt N$, every such scheme inspects more than
$\sqrt N$ objects.

Two logically independent obstructions reach the same magnitude. The
\emph{amplitude barrier}: $r$-tuples over $A\subseteq[1,M]$ realise at most
$rM+1$ distinct sums, so if $rM<p$ every modular collision is trivial and the
scheme cannot factor at all; success forces $p\le rM$. The \emph{span barrier}:
any scheme extracting factors as $\gcd$ of differences of its values, if it
reveals $f$, must produce two values differing by at least $f$. The
\emph{coverage barrier}: a fixed scheme with $k$ search points and values below
$B$ reveals at most $\log_P(B)\,k^2$ primes $\ge P$. Once the counting and
amplitude barriers are cleared, $r$-SUM collision factoring provably outputs
the factor. The exponent improvement is real and confined to storage; the
barrier does not move.
\end{abstract}

